---
title: "Shadow DOM"
description: "$framework Data Grid can render the grid in a shadow root of its own, so the page's CSS cannot restyle it."
frameworks: ["javascript"]
---

Rendering the grid in a shadow root stops the page's CSS from interfering with it. Use this when page styles change how the grid's controls look, such as its filter inputs and buttons.

## Isolating the Grid from Page Styles

{% gridExampleRunner title="Page Styles" name="page-styles" /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    enableShadowDom: true,
};
```

In this example:

- The page has `button:not(#toolbar button)` and `input:not(#toolbar input)` rules. The ID gives them enough weight to outrank the grid's styles, so they restyle the floating filter inputs and pagination buttons.
- Check `enableShadowDom` to recreate the grid in a shadow root, and the inputs and buttons go back to the grid's own styling. `enableShadowDom` is an initial property, so changing it needs a new grid.

Page CSS that refers to the grid's elements, such as rules for the classes set by `cellClass`, `rowClass` or a cell component, does not reach a grid in a shadow root. [Theme parameters](./theming-parameters/), CSS variables, [theme modes](./theming-colors/#theme-modes) set on `<html>` or `<body>`, and the inline styles from `cellStyle` and `getRowStyle` still apply. If the grid is created before its container is added to the page, or is moved afterwards, theme mode changes may not apply.

{% note %}
`enableShadowDom` does not apply when using a legacy theme, `themeStyleContainer` or `popupParent`. These put the grid's styles or popups in the page, where they would not work with the grid in a shadow root. With [development validations](./dev-validation/) enabled, the grid logs a warning when `enableShadowDom: true` is ignored.
{% /note %}

## Automatic Detection

When `enableShadowDom` is not set, the grid uses a shadow root only on known pages whose CSS breaks its controls, such as some AI chat visualisations. Set `enableShadowDom: false` to keep the grid in the page's DOM on those pages. A grid you place in your own shadow DOM does not need this option. See [Themed grids in Shadow DOM](./theming/#themed-grids-in-shadow-dom--web-components) for how theme styles reach it.

